-- THE CLEAN HALF OF THE UNDECIDABILITY WITNESS (#1455 round 5).  Its twin is
-- `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/record_multi_variant_shared_access/`, which is #1469's
-- self-draining pin.
--
-- 🚨 WHAT THE PAIR PROVES, AND WHY IT HAD TO BE A PAIR IN THE CORPUS.  Both fixtures
-- import a `zshared.mdk` that is BYTE-IDENTICAL between them (`cmp` the two files),
-- both CONSTRUCT both of `Shared`'s constructors, and both print `t=1,3`.  The ONLY
-- difference is here, at a use site:
--
--     this cell   `mkSA.sx` projected; `mkSB` consumed by `match`   native ok, wasm ok
--     the twin    `mkSA.sx` AND `mkSB.sx` both projected            native ok, wasm TRAPS
--                                                                   (`illegal cast`)
--
-- Two programs with the same declarations, the same field names, the same offsets and
-- the same output, and one of them is broken on a shipping backend.  So no
-- declaration-level rule at the typecheck seam can separate them, and
-- `T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT` is necessarily an approximation rather than the exact
-- characterization of the divergent set — a claim two rounds of #1455 made in some
-- form and two rounds of review measured false.  `stampableFieldRecord`
-- (`compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`) cites these two directories by name.
--
-- ⚠️ ROUNDS 3 AND 4 CITED A PAIR THAT DID NOT HAVE THE PROPERTY — `|A{x,y}|B{x,y,z}|`
-- against `|A{x}|B{x}|`, which share neither their declarations nor their field
-- names, under a sentence asserting they shared both.  Putting the witness in the
-- corpus instead of in prose is the fix: the shared half is now literally shared, and
-- a `cmp` settles it.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS CELL MUST STAY WasmGC-CLEAN.  It is not ledgered in
-- `test/engine_divergence.txt` — deliberately, because its whole value is being the
-- arm that WORKS.  If `diff_compiler_engines` ever reports a divergence here, the
-- pair has stopped being a witness and the argument above needs re-deriving, not
-- silencing with a ledger row.
--
-- Expected output, DERIVED BY HAND: `mkSA = SA { sx = 1, … }` so `mkSA.sx` is 1;
-- `mkSB = SB { sx = 3, … }` and `sxOf` returns whichever arm matches, i.e. 3.  So
-- `t=1,3` — the same bytes the twin prints, which is what makes the two comparable.
import zshared.{Shared, SA, SB, mkSA, mkSB}

-- The `match` the diagnostic's own `help` tells users to write, and the reason this
-- arm is wasm-clean: a `match` binds the field per CONSTRUCTOR, so each arm's cast is
-- to the struct type the value actually has.
sxOf : Shared -> Int
sxOf s = match s
  SA { sx = v } => v
  SB { sx = v } => v

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn ("t=" ++ intToString mkSA.sx ++ "," ++ intToString (sxOf mkSB))
